Relevant columns and their potential usage  
• "country_code": restrict the data set to U.S. publications (value = 'US').  
• "abstract_localized": JSON array that contains the abstract text; needs to be flattened to look for the phrase ‘internet of things’.  
• "filing_date": YYYYMMDD numeric field that tells when the application was filed; extract year & month for the time series (2008‑2022).  
• "publication_number": simple identifier that lets you preview which records you are working with.  
• "application_kind": optional extra filter (e.g. ‘A’ = patent application) if you want only a certain application type.  
• "publication_date": can be inspected to make sure the record has actually been published.  
• "family_id": useful if you eventually want to aggregate by patent family instead of by application.  

Typical conditions that may be used  
• WHERE "country_code" = 'US'  
• LATERAL FLATTEN(input => "abstract_localized") f  
• WHERE LOWER(f.value:"text"::STRING) LIKE '%internet%of%things%'  
• WHERE "filing_date" BETWEEN 20080101 AND 20221231  

Below are ten exploratory Snowflake SQL queries—each limited to 20 rows—progressing from simple inspection to the fully‑formed aggregation that would answer the question.  All column names are enclosed in double quotes.

```sql
-- 1. Peek at a few U.S. publications and their filing dates
SELECT "publication_number",
       "country_code",
       "filing_date"
FROM   PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS
WHERE  "country_code" = 'US'
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Check distinct filing years that exist in the table
SELECT DISTINCT SUBSTR("filing_date"::STRING, 1, 4) AS "filing_year"
FROM   PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS
WHERE  "country_code" = 'US'
ORDER  BY "filing_year"
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Look at the JSON structure stored in "abstract_localized"
SELECT "publication_number",
       f.value AS "abstract_json"
FROM   PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS t,
       LATERAL FLATTEN(input => t."abstract_localized") f
WHERE  "country_code" = 'US'
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Extract the abstract text string for easy reading
SELECT "publication_number",
       f.value:"language"::STRING   AS "lang",
       f.value:"text"::STRING       AS "abstract_text"
FROM   PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS t,
       LATERAL FLATTEN(input => t."abstract_localized") f
WHERE  "country_code" = 'US'
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 5. First fuzzy search: abstracts that mention the phrase (case‑insensitive, whitespace wild‑carded)
SELECT "publication_number",
       f.value:"text"::STRING AS "abstract_text"
FROM   PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS t,
       LATERAL FLATTEN(input => t."abstract_localized") f
WHERE  "country_code" = 'US'
  AND  LOWER(f.value:"text"::STRING) LIKE '%internet%of%things%'
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Add the filing date and restrict to 2008‑2022
SELECT "publication_number",
       "filing_date",
       f.value:"text"::STRING AS "abstract_text"
FROM   PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS t,
       LATERAL FLATTEN(input => t."abstract_localized") f
WHERE  "country_code" = 'US'
  AND  LOWER(f.value:"text"::STRING) LIKE '%internet%of%things%'
  AND  "filing_date" BETWEEN 20080101 AND 20221231
ORDER  BY "filing_date"
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Derive year‑month (YYYY‑MM) for those filings
SELECT TO_CHAR(TO_DATE("filing_date"::STRING, 'YYYYMMDD'), 'YYYY‑MM') AS "filing_month",
       COUNT(*) AS "filing_count"
FROM   PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS t,
       LATERAL FLATTEN(input => t."abstract_localized") f
WHERE  "country_code" = 'US'
  AND  LOWER(f.value:"text"::STRING) LIKE '%internet%of%things%'
  AND  "filing_date" BETWEEN 20080101 AND 20221231
GROUP  BY "filing_month"
ORDER  BY "filing_month"
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Same as (7) but deduplicate by simple family, if desired
SELECT TO_CHAR(TO_DATE(MIN("filing_date")::STRING, 'YYYYMMDD'), 'YYYY‑MM') AS "family_filing_month",
       COUNT(DISTINCT t."family_id")                                 AS "family_count"
FROM   PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS t,
       LATERAL FLATTEN(input => t."abstract_localized") f
WHERE  "country_code" = 'US'
  AND  LOWER(f.value:"text"::STRING) LIKE '%internet%of%things%'
  AND  "filing_date" BETWEEN 20080101 AND 20221231
GROUP  BY "family_filing_month"
ORDER  BY "family_filing_month"
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Generate a month calendar (2008‑01 through 2022‑12) and left‑join it to the counts
WITH calendar AS (
    SELECT TO_CHAR(DATEADD(month, seq4(), '2008‑01‑01'::DATE), 'YYYY‑MM') AS "month_key"
    FROM   TABLE(GENERATOR(ROWCOUNT => 180))   -- 15 years * 12 months = 180
), iot AS (
    SELECT TO_CHAR(TO_DATE("filing_date"::STRING, 'YYYYMMDD'), 'YYYY‑MM') AS "month_key",
           COUNT(*) AS "filings"
    FROM   PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS p,
           LATERAL FLATTEN(input => p."abstract_localized") f
    WHERE  p."country_code" = 'US'
      AND  LOWER(f.value:"text"::STRING) LIKE '%internet%of%things%'
      AND  p."filing_date" BETWEEN 20080101 AND 20221231
    GROUP  BY "month_key"
)
SELECT c."month_key",
       COALESCE(i."filings", 0) AS "iot_filings"
FROM   calendar c
LEFT   JOIN iot i ON c."month_key" = i."month_key"
ORDER  BY c."month_key"
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 10. All‑in‑one query to produce IoT filing counts per month (full result, no LIMIT here)
WITH calendar AS (
    SELECT TO_CHAR(DATEADD(month, seq4(), '2008‑01‑01'::DATE), 'YYYY‑MM') AS "month_key"
    FROM   TABLE(GENERATOR(ROWCOUNT => 180))   -- 2008‑01 through 2022‑12
), iot AS (
    SELECT TO_CHAR(TO_DATE("filing_date"::STRING, 'YYYYMMDD'), 'YYYY‑MM') AS "month_key",
           COUNT(*) AS "filings"
    FROM   PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS p,
           LATERAL FLATTEN(input => p."abstract_localized") f
    WHERE  p."country_code" = 'US'
      AND  LOWER(f.value:"text"::STRING) LIKE '%internet%of%things%'
      AND  p."filing_date" BETWEEN 20080101 AND 20221231
    GROUP  BY "month_key"
)
SELECT c."month_key",
       COALESCE(i."filings", 0) AS "iot_filings"
FROM   calendar c
LEFT   JOIN iot i ON c."month_key" = i."month_key"
ORDER  BY c."month_key";
